            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice GINSBURG, justice Ginsburg stated that damages would run only from the alleged unlawful arrest until arraignment, and that without an arraignment no damages would be attributed."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d714a1b62f8b16071d355dabd116723dfb542eb6b21236b2b2225b86a0666e5b",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d714a1b62f8b-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 40,
              "start": 40
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Justice Stevens, justice Stevens expressed concern that the rule might create an incentive for defendants to file \u00a71983 actions in every criminal case."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Mr. FLAXMAN",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d714a1b62f8b16071d355dabd116723dfb542eb6b21236b2b2225b86a0666e5b",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d714a1b62f8b-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 22,
              "start": 21
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Petitioner, through Mr. Flaxman, argued that the Seventh Circuit view is that the cause of action starts at arrest or at arraignment (or a Gerstein hearing)."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Ms. SOLOMON",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "d714a1b62f8b16071d355dabd116723dfb542eb6b21236b2b2225b86a0666e5b",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-d714a1b62f8b-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 41,
              "start": 41
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Respondent, through Ms. Solomon, argued that delayed accrual undermines the statute of limitations and that victims of Fourth Amendment violations have a right to damages."
      }
    ],
    "heading": "Oral Argument",
    "status": "complete",
